The commonest error is a unit mistake: mixing mass and mole, gauge and absolute pressure, or kJ and kJ/kg. The second is a wrong basis: forgetting to choose one, or changing it halfway through.

Other common errors are:
not checking that a balance closes;
using a steady-state equation for a batch or start-up case;
confusing equilibrium with rate;
ignoring the second law when a calculated duty looks too good;
using a correlation outside the range of data it was fitted to;
trusting software output because it looks precise.

State your assumptions. An examiner, like a plant manager, wants to see how you reached the answer, not only the answer.
